<div class="ssn">Halachah 1 (on LM I:60, "Pasach R' Shimon")</div>

<div class="p">Yaakov = yirah = subduing "vain beauty" entirely → meriting ultimate wealth (inheritance without boundaries = all four directions). Arvis is reshus [optional] because through Yaakov's yirah one attains the hisbonenus (contemplation) of R' Shimon bar Yochai, who was exempt from prayer since "his Torah was his craft." Not merely that one's profession is Torah learning — but that one's very wealth and sustenance flow directly from Torah (like the chips of the Tablets that enriched Moshe). Nighttime is inherently conducive to this hisbonenus and to wealth. But nowadays — ha'idana — no one reaches this level even at night, so Arvis has been established as obligatory.</div>

<div class="p">The four berachos of Arvis (two before and two after Krias Shma) correspond to the four directions of Yaakov's unbounded inheritance (vs. only three at Shacharis — for Avraham had not yet conquered the north, where the Sitra Achra holds). The fourth berachah is Hashkiveinu — where we request "spread over us Your shelter of peace" (= Yaakov = Sukkos) and ask about the awakening of sleep, through which one merits yirah.</div>

<div class="ssn">Halachah 2 (on LM I:19, "Tefilah l'Chavakuk")</div>

<div class="p">Three daily prayers = shnayim mikra v'echad targum: two by day (Written Torah = light) and one at night (Targum = Oral Torah = night/sleep). Yaakov's essential work: repairing Lashon Targum (Aramaic = Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil) to build the perfection of Lashon HaKodesh. Through subduing Lavan the Aramean (= Targum), he extracted Leah and Rachel (= Chavah-Woman = perfected Holy Tongue), established twelve Shevatim (twelve oxen supporting Malchus), and seventy souls (subduing the seventy nations). His message to Esav — "I dwelt with Lavan" — declares victory: having broken Targum's evil, Esav falls automatically ("tongue falls upon tongue"). The birth of Yosef (= shemiras haBris) from Rachel daughter of Lavan proves the completion.</div>

<div class="p">Arvis = Targum = the large ayin of Shma (roshei teivos of Shacharis/Minchah/Arvis) = Tree of Knowledge = Sukkos (where the seventy nations diminish). Arvis is reshus because Targum occupies the domain of the permitted (reshus) — between the holy (mitzvah/prohibition) and its opposite — in the zone of Nogah. Chatzos: the night splits into two halves (Tree of Knowledge = good and evil). Through tikunim in the first half (Arvis, Krias Shma, Krias Shma al HaMitah) we subdue the evil and clarify the good, so the second half transforms to pure goodness and subsumes into day. Dovid HaMelech was careful with chatzos = perfection of Malchus. Marital union after midnight = Chavah-Woman built through repairing the Tree of Knowledge.</div>

<div class="ssn">Halachah 3 (on LM I:52, "HaNe'or BaLailah")</div>

<div class="p">The philosophical core: heretics claim the world is a "necessary reality" (mechuyav hametzius). In truth, the world is a "possible reality" (efshari hametzius) — only after the souls of Israel were emanated did Hashem become "obligated," as it were, to create it for their sake. Now that Israel exists, the world indeed functions as necessary reality — which is the source of the heretical error (mistaking post-creation reality for pre-creation ontology).</div>

<div class="p">Arvis = reshus = possible reality. Prayer = creation of the world (the Word of Hashem through which heaven and earth were made; "Ad-nai, open my lips"). At night = before creation = darkness = incomprehensible depth ("He placed darkness as His concealment"). Before Israel's souls were emanated, the world was only possible — Hashem's "prayer" was reshus (no obligation to speak creation into being). Yaakov established Arvis because "Israel arose in thought first" — at that primordial moment of thought, before actual emanation, the world was still possible. By morning (= day = the light of creation), when souls have been revealed, prayer becomes obligatory — Hashem is "compelled" to create for Israel's sake.</div>

<div class="p">The nations' hold: Yishma'el and Esav grasp only Avraham and Yitzchak (= the post-creation state, where the world is a necessary reality — from which their heresy derives). In Yaakov (= Israel that arose in thought first), where the world is still a possible reality, they have no hold whatsoever. The rearrangement b'hibar'am = b'Avraham points to this transition: Bereishis-He created (possible reality, for Israel's sake) → b'hibar'am (necessary reality, once Israel was actually emanated). The essential perfection is indeed the daytime state of necessary reality — but for now, the nations have a hold there, while at the root of Yaakov they have none at all. R' Nosson closes with a rare double plea: "Hashem HaTov yechaper" — twice.</div>

